1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:08,180
When you drop two balls from the same height, they always hit the ground at the same speed, right?

2
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:15,980
This is a phenomenon that seems obvious, as demonstrated in Galileo's time.

3
00:00:16,939 --> 00:00:28,460
This can be derived from the law of conservation of energy, which states that the sum of potential energy and kinetic energy is always constant.

4
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:33,820
But why is energy conserved?

5
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:41,500
Since it's called a law, it might seem like something that's just taken for granted in physics.

6
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:52,640
Actually, hidden behind this obvious fact lies symmetry, one of the most important concepts in physics.

7
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:58,520
Noether's theorem, which connects symmetry and conservation laws.

8
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:01,460
Let's explore it together.

9
00:01:04,159 --> 00:01:06,340
The answer to this question comes from

10
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:11,320
Noether's theorem, discovered by mathematician Emmy Noether.

11
00:01:12,380 --> 00:01:16,300
What Noether discovered can be expressed in just one sentence.

12
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:20,860
Yet it's a revolutionary and fundamental theorem.

13
00:01:21,780 --> 00:01:24,460
Where there is symmetry, there is a conservation law.

14
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,200
That might not make sense just yet.

15
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:31,420
Let's now unravel the meaning of this theorem

16
00:01:32,070 --> 00:01:34,840
using free fall, the simplest example.

17
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:37,780
Let's start with an experiment.

18
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:42,480
We place a ball at height H above the ground.

19
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:47,340
During the fall, two types of energy are changing.

20
00:01:48,439 --> 00:01:50,180
First, potential energy.

21
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:55,000
The higher something is, the more potential energy it has.

22
00:01:55,980 --> 00:01:58,600
In formula, it's mgh.

23
00:02:00,319 --> 00:02:02,080
And then kinetic energy.

24
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:06,080
The faster something moves, the more energy it has.

25
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:10,979
In formula, it's ½mv².

26
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:15,480
As it falls, potential energy decreases

27
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:18,000
and kinetic energy increases.

28
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:22,840
But the sum of these two always remains constant.

29
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:30,120
The sum of potential energy and kinetic energy doesn't change during motion.

30
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,520
This is the law of conservation of energy.

31
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:41,340
When it hits the ground, potential energy becomes zero and all of it becomes kinetic energy.

32
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:47,300
At this point, the velocity can be calculated as √2gh.

33
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:52,100
But why is energy conserved?

34
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:55,580
This is where today's main topic begins.

35
00:02:57,300 --> 00:03:01,660
Let's think about a simple but important fact.

36
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,360
Let's drop this ball at midnight today.

37
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:11,000
The velocity when it hits the ground is √2gh.

38
00:03:12,420 --> 00:03:14,740
What about tomorrow at 6 AM?

39
00:03:15,940 --> 00:03:18,240
Needless to say, the result is the same.

40
00:03:18,940 --> 00:03:21,960
No matter what time you do the experiment, the result doesn't change.

41
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,240
The result is unaffected by when you do it.

42
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:29,240
This is time symmetry.

43
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:36,300
Using slightly more technical language, "getting the same result no matter when"

44
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,040
can be described as being symmetric with respect to time.

45
00:03:41,780 --> 00:03:44,900
You might think that's obvious.

46
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:53,440
But Noether's theorem tells us that this time symmetry is what gives rise to energy conservation.

47
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:58,140
The result was the same no matter when we changed the time.

48
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:02,420
What happens if we change the height?

49
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:09,060
When dropped from height H, the velocity was √2gh.

50
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:16,100
If dropped from a slightly higher position, the velocity when hitting the ground increases slightly.

51
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:21,320
Wait, the result changes when the height is different?

52
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:26,580
That means there's no symmetry in the vertical direction.

53
00:04:27,640 --> 00:04:28,260
Why is that?

54
00:04:29,520 --> 00:04:32,160
It's because of Earth's gravity.

55
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:37,800
Earth's gravity changes potential energy depending on height.

56
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:42,320
So space is not uniform in the vertical direction.

57
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:45,300
But wait a moment.

58
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:48,920
Let's shift the ball horizontally.

59
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:53,920
Since gravity acts uniformly straight down,

60
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:57,460
the result of the fall doesn't change when shifted sideways.

61
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:02,139
This means there is symmetry in the horizontal direction.

62
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:08,360
Gravity breaks the spatial symmetry in the vertical direction.

63
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:15,000
And in directions without symmetry, there is no corresponding conservation law.

64
00:05:15,900 --> 00:05:21,340
Conversely, conservation laws arise where there is symmetry.

65
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:25,740
Now let's organize what we've learned so far.

66
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:31,600
In free fall, the result is the same no matter when you drop it.

67
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:42,260
That is, there is time symmetry. And the sum of potential energy and kinetic energy is constant.

68
00:05:43,419 --> 00:05:52,580
How are these two facts connected? Noether's theorem explains this.

69
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:59,660
We'll use a little math, but I'll explain it so you can grasp just the concept.

70
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:06,620
To arrive at Noether's theorem, let's introduce a function called the Lagrangian.

71
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:12,780
This Lagrangian contains all the information about an object's motion.

72
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:18,980
Skipping the derivation, the Lagrangian is calculated as follows.

73
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:24,520
T is kinetic energy, V is potential energy.

74
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:30,040
The Lagrangian L is the difference between these two energies.

75
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:34,220
You might wonder why subtraction?

76
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:39,100
For today, think of it as a convenient function that calculates

77
00:06:39,670 --> 00:06:44,040
the difference between kinetic and potential energy to describe motion.

78
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:51,000
Once the Lagrangian is determined, we can calculate exactly how an object moves.

79
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:55,459
Let's look at the Lagrangian for free fall.

80
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:02,840
As the ball falls, you can see how the value of this Lagrangian L changes.

81
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:09,980
But how does the Lagrangian connect to the object's motion?

82
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:17,240
I said that once the Lagrangian is determined, we can calculate exactly how an object moves.

83
00:07:17,990 --> 00:07:25,500
What connects the Lagrangian to motion is an equation called the Euler-Lagrange equation.

84
00:07:26,500 --> 00:07:30,240
It looks a bit complex, but we won't go deep into it today.

85
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:44,540
However, when you put the free fall Lagrangian into this equation, you get acceleration a = -g, just like Newton's equation of motion.

86
00:07:45,700 --> 00:08:00,000
For simple motion like free fall, there's little merit in using the Lagrangian and Euler-Lagrange equation, but this method is convenient when dealing with more complex motion.

87
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:20,720
For now, just remember that the Lagrangian is a function containing all information about motion, and once you know it, you can mechanically calculate equations of motion by substituting it into the Euler-Lagrange equation.

88
00:08:22,460 --> 00:08:28,020
Now let's see how the Lagrangian relates to symmetry.

89
00:08:29,380 --> 00:08:31,920
Let's recall time symmetry.

90
00:08:33,300 --> 00:08:36,400
The result was the same no matter when we did the experiment.

91
00:08:37,599 --> 00:08:39,340
Here are two balls.

92
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:43,800
We drop the first one and it lands.

93
00:08:45,140 --> 00:08:49,920
Now let's drop the second ball from the same height after a time delay.

94
00:08:51,460 --> 00:08:55,820
Look. The two curves overlap completely.

95
00:08:57,120 --> 00:09:07,720
The Lagrangian changes during the fall because the height changes, but the pattern of change in the Lagrangian is exactly the same for both balls.

96
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:11,860
In other words, when you drop it doesn't matter.

97
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:17,780
The Lagrangian is determined only by the time elapsed since the fall began.

98
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:23,040
The Lagrangian doesn't directly contain time t.

99
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:30,720
That's because both gravitational acceleration g and mass m are the same whenever you measure them.

100
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:35,560
This is the mathematical identity of time symmetry.

101
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:43,160
The Lagrangian not depending on time and having time symmetry mean the same thing.

102
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:50,299
Now let's see how conserved quantities emerge from symmetry.

103
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:55,340
Consider the equation of the Lagrangian differentiated with respect to time.

104
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:59,040
Pay attention to the last term.

105
00:10:00,400 --> 00:10:06,760
This represents how much the Lagrangian directly depends on time.

106
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:10,280
When there is time symmetry,

107
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:14,120
that is, when the result is the same no matter when you experiment,

108
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:16,680
this term becomes zero.

109
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:21,140
Calculating under that condition,

110
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:23,480
we get this equation.

111
00:10:24,820 --> 00:10:27,060
The time derivative being zero means

112
00:10:27,520 --> 00:10:30,460
the contents of the brackets don't change over time.

113
00:10:31,480 --> 00:10:34,080
In other words, a conserved quantity exists.

114
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:38,240
Let's verify what this conserved quantity actually is.

115
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:43,520
Substituting the free fall Lagrangian and calculating,

116
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:49,900
½mq'² is kinetic energy,

117
00:10:50,700 --> 00:10:58,320
and mgq is potential energy. You can see it's the sum of kinetic and potential energy.

118
00:10:59,279 --> 00:11:08,460
This is exactly energy. What's important here is that we didn't use any experimental results or physical intuition.

119
00:11:09,500 --> 00:11:18,400
The conservation of energy is derived solely from the mathematical condition that the Lagrangian doesn't change with time.

120
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:23,560
Conservation laws necessarily follow from symmetry.

121
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,800
This is the power of Noether's theorem.

122
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:30,460
And what makes Noether's theorem remarkable

123
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:34,860
is that she proved this holds for all symmetries.

124
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:38,920
For example, let's consider spatial symmetry.

125
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:43,560
When the Lagrangian doesn't depend on position x,

126
00:11:44,060 --> 00:11:48,940
when two objects of equal mass collide, their velocities are exchanged.

127
00:11:49,960 --> 00:11:54,000
But the sum of the two momenta always remains constant.

128
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:57,200
This is the conservation of momentum.

129
00:11:58,360 --> 00:12:01,320
Let's look at another one: rotational symmetry.

130
00:12:02,380 --> 00:12:08,579
When the Lagrangian doesn't depend on angle θ, like Earth orbiting the Sun,

131
00:12:09,240 --> 00:12:15,140
Earth moves faster when close and slower when far from the Sun.

132
00:12:16,380 --> 00:12:20,600
But the area swept in equal times is always the same.

133
00:12:21,860 --> 00:12:27,600
This is Kepler's second law, also known as the conservation of angular momentum.

134
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:30,680
They all have the same structure.

135
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:40,060
Today we've seen that shifting in time, shifting sideways, or rotating doesn't change the result.

136
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:48,460
Such symmetries that remain unchanged under shifting operations always have corresponding conserved quantities.

137
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:53,760
These conserved quantities are called Noether charges.

138
00:12:54,940 --> 00:13:01,640
Energy, momentum, and angular momentum are all specific examples of Noether charges.

139
00:13:03,100 --> 00:13:07,060
Noether's theorem plays an active role in even broader domains.

140
00:13:07,960 --> 00:13:11,940
In relativity, time and space are treated in a unified way.

141
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:16,759
There, energy and momentum are also combined,

142
00:13:17,180 --> 00:13:25,460
and from spacetime symmetry, conservation laws for a physical quantity called the energy-momentum tensor are derived.

143
00:13:26,680 --> 00:13:33,840
In particle physics, charge conservation is derived from a special symmetry called gauge symmetry.

144
00:13:35,020 --> 00:13:41,439
Noether's theorem is a very important foundation that plays a role in various areas of physics.

145
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:56,280
In today's video, we introduced the world of Noether's theorem—simple yet profoundly deep—which states that where there is symmetry, there is a conservation law.

146
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:03,220
I hope you got a sense of the fascinating physics hidden behind the obvious.

147
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:07,400
Until the next video, take care!

